Two source subagents held disjoint paths. Root owned shared module wiring, Git, publication, and serial offline compilation and browser execution; a third agent independently reviewed source, actual evidence, seals and committed blobs. Both old raw/graph/factory holds remain ACTIVE. Only the two selected new source leases are explicitly released after publication.

RR18b1 counts canvas JSON and its quoted ABI envelope before reserving output, preserves legacy wire bytes, compares canonical model bytes without a second owned model string, and shares immutable admitted frame buffers. Dispatch also avoids unconditional before/after model serialization. The reproduced baseline requested a 2,097,152-byte allocation before refusing geometry; the repaired refusal passes a 65,536-byte maximum allocation-request assertion in that measured apply window. This does not bound whole-app heap or RSS. Requests still own bounded canonical keys, exports still copy, and caching can increase retained memory.

All 60 default Rust cases, scoped formatting, Clippy and fresh release WASM pass. Actual browser controls at 320 and 1440 px exercise canvas layout, compute/apply, rotation with exact model preservation, history, geometry import and model staging/refusal. One retained browser locator failure was narrowly corrected without repeating unchanged Rust suites. The published head has two commits, 91 sealed evidence files and 4,696 committed input bindings.

RR18b2 retains presentation drafts across fallback/recover, including focus, backward caret, creation/import/geometry drafts and disclosure state. It adds no model JSON copy/hash or semantic engine. Retained content and metadata are bounded at 64 fields, 32 disclosures, 1 MiB UTF-8 and 2 MiB UTF-16. Fresh acknowledged import or confirmed discard advances document context; identical stable IDs in another document cannot inherit old drafts. Quota refusal keeps the connected DOM. A mandatory cancellation barrier and callback ownership prevent stale work from publishing when quota blocks font cancellation; encoding/decoding faults keep the barrier. These bounds do not bound DOM traversal or native allocations. A preflush layout revision may safely require retry.

Independent review found another real usability bug after the first source commit: importing and selecting a valid 214-character element ID blocked later actions on immutable metadata. Root reproduced it through normal controls. A focused second source commit retains and charges the full exact selected identity only when inspector records need it. Bounded weak references to fresh native disclosure defaults preserve explicit closed-state choices without retaining detached DOM. Resetting mutable drafts/disclosures or choosing Document root provides an actual quota escape. No identity hashing, truncation or silent inspector-draft omission is used. The third commit contains final evidence; the original implementation remains in history.

On the final loader, five focused scripts pass six long-ID journeys (214, 200014 and 600014 characters at both widths), both recovery journeys, all 12 boundary guards, the cancellation race and both output-fault cases. Three further scripts pass ordinary uninstrumented recovery/export/keyboard, phase observations and identity checks. Ordinary recovery preserves exact model, canvas, history, drafts and caret, and downloads 900381 JSON bytes through a 2701179-byte URI. The evidence has 330 sealed files and 4,706 committed input bindings. Qualified unchanged #112 Rust WASM is explicitly reused; fresh Rust tests or builds are not claimed for this JavaScript-only change.

