## Current performance policy: workload ranges, parity first

The user superseded the universal 50 ms timing gate on 2026-10-28. [performance-policy.json](performance-policy.json) is now authoritative for all active briefs and historical execution-limit references. Initial event-to-visible target bands are 16–50 ms for continuous feedback, 50–150 ms for ordinary authoring, 150–350 ms for named dense fixtures, and 250–1000 ms for named large import/export/recovery. These are engineering targets pending matched-native calibration; faster results are acceptable, and individual excursions require review rather than automatic rejection. Main-thread tasks and end-to-end latency are distinct metrics.

New qualification records two warmups and seven measured samples per affected action/viewport with raw min/median/max; p95 needs at least thirty samples. Preserve every earlier failure and measurement, including 290/287 ms long tasks, without relabelling them as current pass/fail evidence. Correct, usable SysON workflows are the priority. Integrity/admission quotas, exact-data preservation and unusable-control blockers remain unchanged.

